# Experiments

These experiments compare standard LightGlue with motion-pruned LightGlue using SuperPoint features and the TUM RGB-D dataset. All of the following commands can be ran from the project root to replicate the experiments. Ensure all required dependencies are installed.

## Experiment 1: Speed

`experiment_1_speed.py` evaluates whether motion pruning reduces feature matching runtime compared ot standard LightGlue

### How to Run


Rotation-based sampling: 
```bash
python experiments/experiment_1_speed.py --motion rotation --gaps 10 50 100 200 --pairs-per-gap 5 --repeats 2 --out results/experiment1/rotation
```

Random sampling
```bash
python experiments/experiment_1_speed.py --motion random --gaps 10 50 100 200 --pairs-per-gap 5 --repeats 2 --out results/experiment1/random
```

Translation-based sampling
```bash
python experiments/experiment_1_speed.py --motion translation --gaps 10 50 100 200 --pairs-per-gap 5 --repeats 2 --out results/experiment1/translation
```

Each run samples up to 5 image pairs per frame gap and runs each matching method twice per pair. Rotation and translation sampling select pairs across the range of the corresponding camera motion.

This can be ran with the `--help` flag to see the details of each argument it can take.


## Experiment 2: Accuracy (Comparing Match Count)

`experiment_2_accuracy.py` compares the number of matches returned by standard and motion-pruned LightGlue to evalaute whether pruning reduces the number of available matches.

## How to run

```bash
python experiments/experiment_2_accuracy.py --pairs-per-gap 5
```

